---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const outcomes = [
  { metric: '100+', label: 'enterprise migrations, implementations, and deployments supported' },
  { metric: '40%', label: 'faster deployments through repeatable workflows' },
  { metric: '50%', label: 'lower onboarding effort through repeatable workflows and runbooks' },
  { metric: '30%', label: 'lower onboarding escalations through stronger discovery and readiness' },
];

const deliveryFlow = [
  {
    number: '01',
    title: 'Discovery & requirements',
    body: 'Clarify the customer need, implementation context, and decisions that have to be resolved before execution begins.',
  },
  {
    number: '02',
    title: 'Readiness',
    body: 'Turn requirements into practical readiness checks, dependencies, and handoffs so delivery starts with fewer avoidable gaps.',
  },
  {
    number: '03',
    title: 'Implementation planning',
    body: 'Translate the work into repeatable migration, integration, deployment, and onboarding steps that teams can execute consistently.',
  },
  {
    number: '04',
    title: 'Delivery & handoff',
    body: 'Coordinate the path through deployment and customer handoff while keeping documentation and operating expectations clear.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Enterprise Implementation & Migration Delivery | Mike Gilmore"
  description="A public-safe case study of enterprise implementation, migration, onboarding, readiness, and delivery practices across 100+ customer engagements."
>
  <header class="case-hero shell">
    <div class="case-hero__copy">
      <p class="eyebrow">Case study · Enterprise implementation</p>
      <h1>Turning customer requirements into repeatable implementation and migration delivery.</h1>
      <p>
        Across more than 100 enterprise migrations, implementations, and deployments, the work centered on moving from
        discovery and requirements into practical readiness, execution, and customer handoff.
      </p>
      <p class="case-note">Public-safe summary based on substantiated professional experience. Customer identities and proprietary implementation details are intentionally excluded.</p>
    </div>
    <aside class="case-hero__role" aria-label="Role and focus">
      <p class="signal-label">Role pattern</p>
      <p>Customer-facing cloud and implementation delivery</p>
      <p class="signal-label">Focus</p>
      <p>Discovery · onboarding · migration · integration · deployment planning</p>
    </aside>
  </header>

  <section class="case-outcomes" aria-labelledby="outcomes-heading">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="case-section-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--light">Evidence</p>
        <h2 id="outcomes-heading">Repeatability improved both speed and readiness.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="case-metric-grid">
        {outcomes.map((outcome) => (
          <div class="case-metric">
            <strong>{outcome.metric}</strong>
            <span>{outcome.label}</span>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="shell case-story" aria-labelledby="context-heading">
    <aside class="case-story__rail">
      <p class="eyebrow">Context</p>
      <p>Enterprise SaaS delivery</p>
      <p>Municipal and enterprise customers</p>
      <p>2018–2022 experience</p>
    </aside>
    <div class="case-story__body">
      <h2 id="context-heading">The delivery problem was larger than the deployment itself.</h2>
      <p>
        The work spanned discovery, onboarding, migration, integration, and deployment planning. At that scale, successful
        delivery depended on translating requirements into clear implementation plans and practical customer decisions,
        then carrying those decisions through readiness and handoff.
      </p>
      <p>
        The strongest improvement came from making the process more repeatable: stronger discovery, clearer readiness
        checks, standardized workflows, reusable runbooks, and more deliberate stakeholder handoffs.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--soft" aria-labelledby="flow-heading">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="case-section-heading case-section-heading--dark">
        <p class="eyebrow">Delivery pattern</p>
        <h2 id="flow-heading">A simple flow for reducing ambiguity before it becomes delivery friction.</h2>
      </div>
      <ol class="case-flow">
        {deliveryFlow.map((step) => (
          <li>
            <span class="case-flow__number">{step.number}</span>
            <h3>{step.title}</h3>
            <p>{step.body}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="shell case-story case-story--reverse" aria-labelledby="judgment-heading">
    <aside class="case-story__rail">
      <p class="eyebrow">What this demonstrates</p>
      <p>Requirements discovery</p>
      <p>Implementation planning</p>
      <p>Stakeholder coordination</p>
      <p>Process improvement</p>
    </aside>
    <div class="case-story__body">
      <h2 id="judgment-heading">Technical depth mattered most when it supported a delivery decision.</h2>
      <p>
        The role sat between customer needs and technical execution. Cloud infrastructure and integration context helped
        clarify what was practical, but the value was not a list of tools. It was the ability to connect requirements,
        readiness, implementation choices, and operating expectations into a delivery path customers and internal teams
        could act on.
      </p>
      <p>
        Standardizing customer environments and operating practices also reduced cloud costs by 20%, while governance,
        controls, and documentation supported 99.9% uptime and SOC 2 readiness.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--dark" aria-labelledby="takeaway-heading">
    <div class="shell case-takeaway">
      <p class="eyebrow eyebrow--light">Takeaway</p>
      <h2 id="takeaway-heading">Implementation gets easier when discovery, readiness, and technical execution are treated as one delivery system.</h2>
      <p>
        That pattern is the part of the work I carry forward: make the requirements concrete, surface readiness gaps early,
        standardize what can be repeated, and keep customer and technical teams aligned through handoff.
      </p>
      <a class="button button--secondary case-button-light" href="/work/">Back to selected work</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
